[[:db/cas 17592186398702 :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}]=0" "det\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{bmatrix}=0"] [:db/cas 17592186398703 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the unique solution of $[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}]x=0$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the unique solution of $\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{bmatrix}x=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186398704 :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0" "det\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0"] [:db/cas 17592186398705 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ has exactly one solution" "$\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ has exactly one solution"] [:db/cas 17592186398706 :answer/value "${[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}$ is invertible" "${\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}$ is invertible"] [:db/cas 17592186398713 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$"] [:db/cas 17592186398713 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1-(-1) & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1-(-1)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{3}.$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1-(-1) & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1-(-1)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186398746 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$ where ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$ where ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$"] [:db/cas 17592186398746 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is sinusoidal. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined." "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is sinusoidal. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."] [:db/cas 17592186398748 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398749 :answer/value "{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398750 :answer/value "{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398751 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398752 :answer/value "{e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}])" "{e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})"] [:db/cas 17592186398753 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]){e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$ where ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}){e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$ where ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$"] [:db/cas 17592186398753 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is sinusoidal. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined." "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is sinusoidal. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."] [:db/cas 17592186398755 :answer/value "{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398756 :answer/value "{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}])" "{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})"] [:db/cas 17592186398757 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398758 :answer/value "{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398759 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398760 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$"] [:db/cas 17592186398760 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution has the same exponential factor, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined." "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution has the same exponential factor, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."] [:db/cas 17592186398762 :answer/value "{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398763 :answer/value "{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398764 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398765 :answer/value "{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}])" "{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})"] [:db/cas 17592186398766 :answer/value "{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398767 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-t \\\\ -2{t}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$ where ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}3{t}^{2}-t \\\\ -2{t}^{2}+4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$ where ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$"] [:db/cas 17592186398767 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-t \\\\ -2{t}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is a polynomial in $t$ of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2$, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={t}^{2}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined." "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{t}^{2}-t \\\\ -2{t}^{2}+4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is a polynomial in $t$ of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2$, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={t}^{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."] [:db/cas 17592186398769 :answer/value "t[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "t\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398770 :answer/value "{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398771 :answer/value "{t}^{2}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "{t}^{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398772 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398773 :answer/value "{t}^{2}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "{t}^{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398781 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?"] [:db/cas 17592186398781 :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}=[\\begin{aligned}2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} & 2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order." "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}=\\begin{bmatrix}2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} & 2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."] [:db/cas 17592186398783 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-1} & 2{e}^{-1} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-1} & 2{e}^{-1} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-1}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398784 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 2t{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 2t{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398785 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398786 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} & 2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} & 2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398787 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398789 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}e\\operatorname{cos}⁡2 & -e\\operatorname{sin}⁡2 \\\\ e\\operatorname{sin}⁡2 & e\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}e\\operatorname{cos}⁡2 & -e\\operatorname{sin}⁡2 \\\\ e\\operatorname{sin}⁡2 & e\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398790 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398791 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398792 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & -{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & -{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398793 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398809 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186398810 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186398811 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186398812 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186398813 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186398814 :question/problem "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-6x+y \\\\ 12x-2y\\end{aligned}].$" "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-6x+y \\\\ 12x-2y\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186398814 :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 12 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 12 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{6}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]y,\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$" "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 \\\\ 12 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSetting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 \\\\ 12 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{6}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6}y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}y,\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$"] [:db/cas 17592186398816 :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" "{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186398817 :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}]}" "{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186398818 :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" "{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186398819 :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" "{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186398820 :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}" "{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186398975 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is a matrix for which all entries *above* the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHence, it is a lower triangular matrix. \n\nBy contrast, matrices $A$, $C$, $D$, and $E$ each have at least one non-zero entry above the diagonal:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, they are not lower triangular matrices." "Notice that $B$ is a matrix for which all entries *above* the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHence, it is a lower triangular matrix. \n\nBy contrast, matrices $A$, $C$, $D$, and $E$ each have at least one non-zero entry above the diagonal:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,E=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, they are not lower triangular matrices."] [:db/cas 17592186398977 :answer/value "C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398978 :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398979 :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398980 :answer/value "E=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "E=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398981 :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398982 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & k+1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & k+1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & k-2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+6y=4 \\\\  & (k-2)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=2,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y.$ So, we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $2x+6y=4.$  \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=2.$" "The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & k+1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & k+1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & k-2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+6y=4 \\\\  & (k-2)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=2,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y.$ So, we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $2x+6y=4.$  \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186398989 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ 17 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]\\,$, find $\\,T(37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2}).$" "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}21 \\\\ 17 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}\\,$, find $\\,T(37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186398989 :question/worked-solution "First, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2} & =37{v}_{1}+37{v}_{2}+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =37({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =37(0)+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =2{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2})=T(2{v}_{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2{v}_{2}) & =2T({v}_{2}) \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ 17 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ 34 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ 34 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}].$" "First, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2} & =37{v}_{1}+37{v}_{2}+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =37({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =37(0)+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =2{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2})=T(2{v}_{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2{v}_{2}) & =2T({v}_{2}) \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}21 \\\\ 17 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}42 \\\\ 34 \\\\ -28\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}42 \\\\ 34 \\\\ -28\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186398991 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}63 \\\\ 51 \\\\ -42\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}63 \\\\ 51 \\\\ -42\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398992 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-42 \\\\ -34 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-42 \\\\ -34 \\\\ 28\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186398993 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-21 \\\\ 