, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186399575 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186399576 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186399577 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186399578 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186399579 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186399594 :question/problem "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 40 & -13 & 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. From left to right, what are the missing elements from the second row of the matrix $U?$" "$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 40 & -13 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. From left to right, what are the missing elements from the second row of the matrix $U?$"] [:db/cas 17592186399594 :question/worked-solution "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{21}$ of the matrix $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 40 & -13 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 40 & -13 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the elements from the second row of $U$ are $0,3,$ and $9.$" "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{21}$ of the matrix $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 40 & -13 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 40 & -13 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the elements from the second row of $U$ are $0,3,$ and $9.$"] [:db/cas 17592186399601 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$N=[\\begin{aligned}23 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$N=\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186399601 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $N-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nN-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}23-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(N-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}23-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (23-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-0\\cdot (-8) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -23) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =23.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal." "Let's start by writing the matrix $N-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nN-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}23-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(N-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}23-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (23-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-0\\cdot (-8) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -23) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =23.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal."] [:db/cas 17592186399692 :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186399692 :question/worked-solution "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=(-3){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are elementary matrices." "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=(-3){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are elementary matrices."] [:db/cas 17592186399699 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186399699 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(1)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- one $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =-2,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =1.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(1) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A+2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only \n$z$\nis an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A+2I)z=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(1)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- one $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =-2,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =1.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(1) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A+2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only \n$z$\nis an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A+2I)z=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."] [:db/cas 17592186399706 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,z=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186399706 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- one $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =-1,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(2) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A+I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A+I)y=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]=z,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A+I)^{2}y=(A+I)z=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- one $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =-1,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(2) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A+I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A+I)y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}=z,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A+I)^{2}y=(A+I)z=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."] [:db/cas 17592186399713 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection."] [:db/cas 17592186399713 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$" "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186399715 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186399716 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186399717 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186399718 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186399719 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186399720 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection."] [:db/cas 17592186399720 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$" "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of 