gned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,z=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186399753 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(-2).$  \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAmong the given vectors, only $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-(-2)I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=y,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A-(-2)I)^{2}x=(A+2I)y=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(-2).$  \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAmong the given vectors, only $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-(-2)I)x=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=y,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A-(-2)I)^{2}x=(A+2I)y=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."] [:db/cas 17592186400205 :question/problem "Which of the following are diagonal matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\text{ }\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are diagonal matrices?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186400212 :question/problem "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\end{aligned}],\\