**III.** $det(F)=-2$\n\n$$\nF=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $F$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(F)=-7$\n- **II.** $F$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(F)=-2$\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400352 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(D)=1$\n- **III.** $det(D)=0$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(D)=1$\n- **III.** $det(D)=0$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400359 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $S$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $S$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $S$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(S)=5+2\\sqrt{7}$\n\n$$\nS=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{7} & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $S$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $S$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $S$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(S)=5+2\\sqrt{7}$\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{7} & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400366 :question/problem "Given that the determinant of\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & m \\\\ m & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis $-29$, find all the possible values of $m.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $m=-6$\n- **II.** $m=-5$\n- **III.** $m=1$\n- **IV.** $m=3$\n- **V.** $m=4$\n- **VI.** $m=8$" "Given that the determinant of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & m \\\\ m & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nis $-29$, find all the possible values of $m.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $m=-6$\n- **II.** $m=-5$\n- **III.** $m=1$\n- **IV.** $m=3$\n- **V.** $m=4$\n- **VI.** $m=8$"] [:db/cas 17592186400471 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?"] [:db/cas 17592186400471 :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below ${a}_{12}=-2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}.$" "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below ${a}_{12}=-2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186400478 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -6 & -7 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 11 & 10 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 10 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -18 & -7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\nI. ${R}_{2}:=…$\nII. ${R}_{3}:=…$\nIII. ${R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}$\n\neach of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -6 & -7 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 11 & 10 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 10 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -18 & -7 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\nI. ${R}_{2}:=…$\nII. ${R}_{3}:=…$\nIII. ${R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}$\n\neach of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?"] [:db/cas 17592186400478 :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries below ${a}_{13}=3$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -6 & -7 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 11 & 10 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 11 & 10 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 10 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 11 & 10 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 10 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -18 & -7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}.$" "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries below ${a}_{13}=3$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -6 & -7 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 11 & 10 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 11 & 10 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 10 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 11 & 10 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 10 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -18 & -7 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186400485 :question/problem "What is the system of equations represented by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "What is the system of equations represented by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400492 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 10 & 3 & 15\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations \n\nI. ${C}_{1}:=…$\nII. ${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{3}$\nIII. ${C}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 4 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 10 & 3 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations \n\nI. ${C}_{1}:=…$\nII. ${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{3}$\nIII. ${C}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?"] [:db/cas 17592186400492 :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{13}=-2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & -2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 10 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+4{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 10 & 3 & 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+4{C}_{3}.$" "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{13}=-2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 4 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & -2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 10 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+4{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 10 & 3 & 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+4{C}_{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186400499 :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,S=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186400499 :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $P$ and $Q$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $S$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $S$ is an elementary matrix." "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $P$ and $Q$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $S$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $S$ is an elementary matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186400506 :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186400506 :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $A$ and $C$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is an elementary matrix." "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $A$ and $C$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is an elementary matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186400513 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z=4 \\\\ y+2z=-2 \\\\ z=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=4$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2(4)=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=-10$ and $z=4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(-10)-(4)=4\\,⟹\\,x=28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=28$, $y=-10$, and $z=4.$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -1 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -1 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -1 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z=4 \\\\ y+2z=-2 \\\\ z=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=4$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2(4)=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=-10$ and $z=4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(-10)-(4)=4\\,⟹\\,x=28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=28$, $y=-10$, and $z=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186400520 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $A?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $A?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400520 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $2$ pivots in the $1$st and $3$rd columns:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $2$ pivots in the $1$st and $3$rd columns:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400527 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $M$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $M?$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 & 8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The row echelon form matrix $M$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $M?$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 & 8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400527 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $M$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 & 8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $M$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 & 8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400534 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $A?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -9 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $A?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & -9 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400534 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $4$ pivots in the $2$nd, $3$rd, $4$th, and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -9 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $4$ pivots in the $2$nd, $3$rd, $4$th, and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & -9 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400541 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $P$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The row echelon form matrix $P$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400541 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $P$ has $3$ pivots in the $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $1$st column." "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $P$ has $3$ pivots in the $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $1$st column."] [:db/cas 17592186400548 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 9 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 9 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400548 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $2$nd and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 9 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column." "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $2$nd and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 9 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column."] [:db/cas 17592186400590 :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400590 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{4} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns." "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{4} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns."] [:db/cas 17592186400597 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186400597 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form. The system corresponding to $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+0y+7z=-3 \\\\ 0x+y-2z=2 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+7z=-3 \\\\ y-2z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-2z=2\\,⇒\\,y=2z+2\n$$\n\nand from the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx+7z=-3\\,⇒\\,x=-7z-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-7z-3 \\\\ y=2z+2 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7z-3 \\\\ 2z+2 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form. The system corresponding to $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+0y+7z=-3 \\\\ 0x+y-2z=2 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+7z=-3 \\\\ y-2z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-2z=2\\,⇒\\,y=2z+2\n$$\n\nand from the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx+7z=-3\\,⇒\\,x=-7z-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-7z-3 \\\\ y=2z+2 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7z-3 \\\\ 2z+2 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400599 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7z+3 \\\\ 2z-2 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}7z+3 \\\\ 2z-2 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186400600 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7z-3 \\\\ 2z+2 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}-7z-3 \\\\ 2z+2 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186400601 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7z-3 \\\\ 2-2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}7z-3 \\\\ 2-2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186400602 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7z-3 \\\\ 2-2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}-7z-3 \\\\ 2-2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186400603 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3-7z \\\\ 2z+2 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}3-7z \\\\ 2z+2 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186400604 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186400604 :question/worked-solution "The system corresponding to $M$ is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-8y+6z=-4 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x-8y+6z=-4\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx=4y-3z-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4y-3z-2 \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$, $z$ (free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4y-3z-2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The system corresponding to $M$ is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-8y+6z=-4 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x-8y+6z=-4\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means w