 (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186400630 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186400631 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3-4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}3-4y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186400632 :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -9 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -9 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -9 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have two free variables, $y$ and $z.$ That means we can find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement III is true." "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -9 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -9 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -9 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have two free variables, $y$ and $z.$ That means we can find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement III is true."] [:db/cas 17592186400639 :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-1 \\\\ y=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we have $y=4$ and substituting this into the first equation yields $x=-9.$ So,  we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement I is true." "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-1 \\\\ y=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we have $y=4$ and substituting this into the first equation yields $x=-9.$ So,  we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."] [:db/cas 17592186400646 :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=2 \\\\ y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we have $y=-1$ and substituting this into the first equation yields $x=1.$ So,  we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true." "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=2 \\\\ y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we have $y=-1$ and substituting this into the first equation yields $x=1.$ So,  we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true."] [:db/cas 17592186400716 :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$"] [:db/cas 17592186400716 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{2,3}=3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{2,3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{2,3}=3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{2,3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186400718 :answer/value "$\\lambda =1\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\lambda =1\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186400719 :answer/value "$\\lambda =4\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5∓4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\lambda =4\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5∓4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186400720 :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\lambda =3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186400721 :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1∓4\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\lambda =3\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1∓4\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186400722 :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3\\pm 4\\t