| **$5$** | **$6$** | **$9$** | **$13$** | **$25$** |\n| $y$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane  \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 17 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 15 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 8 & 25\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [:db/cas 17592186401132 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 17 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 15 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 8 & 25\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 17 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 15 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 8 & 25\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186401133 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186401134 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 17 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 15 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 25\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 17 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 15 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 25\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186401135 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186401136 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 22 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 17 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 15 & 9 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 13 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 25 & -6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 22 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 17 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 15 & 9 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 13 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 25 & -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186401137 :question/problem "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]?$" "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186401137 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=\\frac{2}{3}.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $\\frac{2}{3}.$" "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=\\frac{2}{3}.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $\\frac{2}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186401144 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ has endpoints at $A(1,1)$ and $B(-1,2).$ What are the coordinates of the corresponding endpoints of $\\overset{―}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}}$, the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T?$" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nA line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ has endpoints at $A(1,1)$ and $B(-1,2).$ What are the coordinates of the corresponding endpoints of $\\overset{―}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}}$, the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T?$"] [:db/cas 17592186401144 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{AB}:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are ${A}^{′}(2,2)$ and ${B}^{′}(-5,7).$" "To find the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{AB}:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are ${A}^{′}(2,2)$ and ${B}^{′}(-5,7).$"] [:db/cas 17592186401179 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401179 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2v-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u+v}(v-u) & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0 \\\\ u & =v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=v$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane." "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2v-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u+v}(v-u) & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0 \\\\ u & =v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=v$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."] [:db/cas 17592186401186 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401186 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6u & 4v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 1-4v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6u-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 6u-4v & =0 \\\\ v & =\\frac{3}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=\\frac{3}{2}u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane." "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}6u & 4v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 1-4v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6u-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 6u-4v & =0 \\\\ v & =\\frac{3}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=\\frac{3}{2}u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."] [:db/cas 17592186401275 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+2w \\\\ v-u \\\\ uw\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+2w \\\\ v-u \\\\ uw\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401275 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=uw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ w & 0 & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ w & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(u-0)+2(0-w) \\\\  & =u-2w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ u-2w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u-2w=0$ defines the plane $u=2w.$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=uw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ w & 0 & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ w & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(u-0)+2(0-w) \\\\  & =u-2w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ u-2w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u-2w=0$ defines the plane $u=2w.$"] [:db/cas 17592186401282 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{u+w} \\\\ uv+w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{u+w} \\\\ uv+w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401282 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={e}^{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={e}^{u+w},\\,z(u,v,w)=uv+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u+w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{u+w} & 0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ v & u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}(0-u{e}^{u+w}) \\\\  & =-u{e}^{2u+w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -u{e}^{2u+w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane." "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={e}^{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={e}^{u+w},\\,z(u,v,w)=uv+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u+w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{u+w} & 0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ v & u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}(0-u{e}^{u+w}) \\\\  & =-u{e}^{2u+w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -u{e}^{2u+w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane."] [:db/cas 17592186401302 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le w\\le 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-3-0) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le w\\le 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-3-0) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401405 :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}xy \\\\ {x}^{2}-y \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} \\\\ \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & -1 \\\\ \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?" "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}xy \\\\ {x}^{2}-y \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}y & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} \\\\ \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & -1 \\\\ \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?"] [:db/cas 17592186401405 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 2x & -1 \\\\ y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, the missing entries are $x,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}y & x \\\\ 2x & -1 \\\\ y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, the missing entries are $x,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$"] [:db/cas 17592186401412 :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xyz} \\\\ x+yz\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & xz{e}^{xyz} & xy{e}^{xyz} \\\\ 1 & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing entries?" "For the transformation $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{xyz} \\\\ x+yz\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & xz{e}^{xyz} & xy{e}^{xyz} \\\\ 1 & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing entries?"] [:db/cas 17592186401412 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xyz})=yz{e}^{xyz},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xyz})=xz{e}^{xyz},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xyz})=xy{e}^{xyz} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+yz)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+yz)=z,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+yz)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}yz{e}^{xyz} & xz{e}^{xyz} & xy{e}^{xyz} \\\\ 1 & z & y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xyz})=yz{e}^{xyz},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xyz})=xz{e}^{xyz},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xyz})=xy{e}^{xyz} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+yz)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+yz)=z,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+yz)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}yz{e}^{xyz} & xz{e}^{xyz} & xy{e}^{xyz} \\\\ 1 & z & y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401419 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,{\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ what type of point is $a?$" "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,{\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ what type of point is $a?$"] [:db/cas 17592186401579 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-2x,\\,2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 32\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(4,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(-4,0),\\,(0,-4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(-4,0)$ | $(0,-4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-15$ | $17$ | $-15$ | $17$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-15$ at the points $(4,0)$ and $(-4,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the points $(0,4)$ and $(0,-4).$" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2x,\\,2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 32\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(4,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(-4,0),\\,(0,-4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(-4,0)$ | $(0,-4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-15$ | $17$ | $-15$ | $17$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-15$ at the points $(4,0)$ and $(-4,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the points $(0,4)$ and $(0,-4).$"] [:db/cas 17592186401688 :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${a}_{22}$ is $-70$, find all the possible values of $k$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2k & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 11 & 9 & k+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Given that the minor of ${a}_{22}$ is $-70$, find all the possible values of $k$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2k & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 11 & 9 & k+3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401688 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and second column:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2k & ∗ & 6 \\\\ ∗ & 5 & ∗ \\\\ 11 & ∗ & k+3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${a}_{22}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}2k & 6 \\\\ 11 & k+3\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}2k & 6 \\\\ 11 & k+3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2k\\cdot (k+3)-11\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k-66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${a}_{22}$ is $-70$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{k}^{2}+6k-66 & =-70 \\\\ 2{k}^{2}+6k+4 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2}+3k+2 & =0 \\\\ (k+1)(k+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=-1$ and $k=-2.$" "First, we remove the second row and second column:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2k & ∗ & 6 \\\\ ∗ & 5 & ∗ \\\\ 11 & ∗ & k+3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${a}_{22}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}2k & 6 \\\\ 11 & k+3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2k & 6 \\\\ 11 & k+3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2k\\cdot (k+3)-11\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k-66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${a}_{22}$ is $-70$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{k}^{2}+6k-66 & =-70 \\\\ 2{k}^{2}+6k+4 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2}+3k+2 & =0 \\\\ (k+1)(k+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=-1$ and $k=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186401848 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186401850 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186401851 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186401852 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186401981 :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $J$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[J\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The inverse of the matrix $J$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[J\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401981 :question/worked-solution "We write down $J$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $J$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[J\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${J}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{J}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We write down $J$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $J$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[J\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${J}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{J}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401988 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($det(A)\\ne 0$), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option I gives a true statement:\n\n- *If $det(A)\\ne 0$ , then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$" "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($det(A)\\ne 0$), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option I gives a true statement:\n\n- *If $det(A)\\ne 0$ , then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$"] [:db/cas 17592186401995 :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $K$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[K\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The inverse of the matrix $K$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[K\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401995 :question/worked-solution "We write down $K$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $K$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[K\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${K}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{K}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We write down $K$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $K$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[K\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${K}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{K}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186402002 :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $D$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${D}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $D$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${D}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186402002 :question/worked-solution "We write down $D$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $D$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${D}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We write down $D$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $D$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${D}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{D}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186402004 :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "{D}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186402005 :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "{D}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186402006 :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "{D}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186402007 :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" "{D}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186402008 :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" "{D}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186402009 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 10 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+10{x}_{3}=-8 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}=6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}={x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 10 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+10{x}_{3}=-8 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}=6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}={x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186402011 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186402012 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8-{x}_{3} \\\\ 3+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}8-{x}_{3} \\\\ 3+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186402013 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-8{x}_{3} \\\\ 6-6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}-8-8{x}_{3} \\\\ 6-6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [