]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$"] [:db/cas 17592186402035 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmat