end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186402115 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}"] [:db/cas 17592186402116 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}"] [:db/cas 17592186402117 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}"] [:db/cas 17592186402118 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}"] [:db/cas 17592186402119 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}"] [:db/cas 17592186402121 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}" "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}"] [:db/cas 17592186402122 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}" "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}"] [:db/cas 17592186402123 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186402124 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186402125 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186402154 :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 3x-y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-7y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 3x-y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-7y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186402156 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186402157 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ only" "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186402158 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ only" "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186402159 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186402160 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186402161 :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=0 \\\\ 2x+5y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-6y+5y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+3\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=0 \\\\ 2x+5y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-6y+5y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+3\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186402163 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ only" "$\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186402164 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186402165 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ only" "$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186402166 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186402167 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186402168 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-2x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-y+xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-2x)-xy)=2-8x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-2x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y+xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y+xy)=-1+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-8x-y & -x \\\\ y & -1+x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-8(0)-0 & -0 \\\\ 0 & -1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-2x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-y+xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-2x)-xy)=2-8x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-2x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y+xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y+xy)=-1+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}2-8x-y & -x \\\\ y & -1+x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2-8(0)-0 & -0 \\\\ 0 & -1+0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186402362 :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -10 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -10 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186402362 :question/worked-solution "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -10 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}41 \\\\ 82\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -10 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\,✓ \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -10 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only ${b}_{2}$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$" "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -10 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}41 \\\\ 82\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -10 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\\,✓ \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -10 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only ${b}_{2}$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186402389 :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186402389 :question/worked-solution "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\,✓ \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-4\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=4\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only ${b}_{1}$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$" "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\\,✓ \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-4\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=4\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only ${b}_{1}$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186402448 :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186402448 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $2$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns." "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $2$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns."] [:db/cas 17592186402461 :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186402461 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns." "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns."] [:db/cas 17592186402468 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each semester: \n\n- $38%$ of the healthy people tend to get sick. This implies that $62%$ of them remain healthy.\n\n- $45%$ of the sick people tend to improve their health. This implies that $55%$ of them remain sick.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Healthy** | **Sick** |\n| To: | **Healthy** | $0.62$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sick** | $0.38$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.62 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.38 & 0.55\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We are given the following situation for each semester: \n\n- $38%$ of the healthy people tend to get sick. This implies that $62%$ of them remain healthy.\n\n- $45%$ of the sick people tend to improve their health. This implies that $55%$ of them remain sick.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Healthy** | **Sick** |\n| To: | **Healthy** | $0.62$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sick** | $0.38$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.62 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.38 & 0.55\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186402484 :answer/value "r=t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "r=t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186402485 :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "r=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186402486 :answer/value "r=t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "r=t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186402487 :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "r=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186402502 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$E=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{aligned}].$" "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186402502 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $E-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nE-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -2 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(E-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -2 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-(-2)\\cdot 8 & =0 \\\\ -12-2\\lambda +6\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $E$ has only one eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ which is a double root of the characteristic equation." "Let's start by writing the matrix $E-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nE-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -2 & -6-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(E-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -2 & -6-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-(-2)\\cdot 8 & =0 \\\\ -12-2\\lambda +6\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $E$ has only one eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ which is a double root of the characteristic equation."] [:db/cas 17592186402509 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{aligned}].$" "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186402509 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -5 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(P-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -5 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-5) & =0 \\\\ 49-7\\lambda -7\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +54 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-14)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 54 \\\\  & =196-216 \\\\  & =-20 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions and therefore $P$ does not have real eigenvalues." "Let's start by writing the matrix $P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -5 & 7-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(P-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -5 & 7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-5) & =0 \\\\ 49-7\\lambda -7\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +54 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-14)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 54 \\\\  & =196-216 \\\\  & =-20 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions and therefore $P$ does not have real eigenvalues."] [:db/cas 17592186402523 :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}].$" "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186402523 :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ using its characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(8)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -24 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ using its characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-6-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(8)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -24 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-(-4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186402525 :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186402526 :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186402527 :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186402528 :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$  and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$  and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186402531 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begi