_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186403817 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186403818 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}